From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ps4-gladius <ps4-gladius@invalid>
Subject: [PATCH] wowprospero: size the secondary arena for the PS4's budget.

thread_budget.h says where the 32 MiB came from: "Every i386 thread has
its own DBT, in memory Wine allocates for it, which on the console is
direct memory: committed as soon as it is allocated, out of up to 12
GiB." That is the PS5. Here these land in the flexible pool, which is 448
MiB in total, and thirty threads at 32 MiB want 960 MiB of it.

What it looks like from outside is not a memory message. The allocation is
refused, pw_wow_thread_fit halves and retries -- which is why every one of
these regions measures 16 MiB and not 32 --

    flexible_holders available=3072K mapped=461024K buckets=29
    flexible_region rank=2 at=7d7bb0000 bytes=16384K prot=0x3 name=orbis
    flexible_region rank=3 at=7d8bb0000 bytes=16384K prot=0x3 name=orbis

-- the pool empties, and the next thread dies in pthread_create with
EAGAIN at about a dozen, three layers away, where it reads as a limit on
threads. src/thread-probe had already shown it is not: a bare title makes
146 threads with the attributes Wine uses.

8 MiB for secondary threads: a quarter of the PS5's, four times the floor
this header already treats as survivable, and these are the threads the
same comment calls an audio mixer or a loader. The first thread's 128 MiB
is untouched -- that is the one a game's hot loop has to fit in, and the
header is explicit that missing it costs a recompile every minute.

⚠ This is a measurement as much as a fix. That those regions are arenas is
inferred -- the address range is place_above_guest's and the count follows
the thread count -- not proven; prot=0x3 means they would be the
read-write half of the engine's write/exec pair. If flexible usage falls
with this, they are arenas. If it does not, the attribution is wrong and
the next step is not the one it looks like.

⚠ And the better answer is still to put these back in direct memory, where
the PS5 keeps them and where this console has gigabytes rather than
megabytes. That change lands in executable memory and the write/exec
alias, which is not where to go while the consumer is still an inference.

⚠ PW_WOW_PS4_BUDGET comes from scripts/prx-ps4.sh rather than __PROSPERO__,
which upstream means a PS5 -- the console the 12 GiB was written for.
diff --git a/wine/wowprospero/thread_budget.h b/wine/wowprospero/thread_budget.h
index a6bec81..3ab8cd0 100644
--- a/wine/wowprospero/thread_budget.h
+++ b/wine/wowprospero/thread_budget.h
@@ -32,7 +32,46 @@ enum {
     PW_WOW_MIN_ENTRIES = 1024,
 };
 #define PW_WOW_FIRST_ARENA ((size_t)128 << 20)
+#ifdef PW_WOW_PS4_BUDGET
+/* ⚠ 12 GiB is the PS5's number, and this console's is 448 MiB.
+ *
+ * The comment above is written for a PS5, where a thread's DBT comes out of
+ * direct memory "out of up to 12 GiB" and 32 MiB a thread is nothing. On the
+ * PS4 these land in the flexible pool, which is 448 MiB in total, and the
+ * arithmetic stops working: thirty threads at 32 MiB want 960 MiB of a 448 MiB
+ * budget.
+ *
+ * What that looks like from the outside is not a memory message at all. The
+ * allocation is refused, pw_wow_thread_fit halves and retries -- which is why
+ * every one of these regions measures 16 MiB and not 32 --
+ *
+ *     flexible_holders available=3072K mapped=461024K buckets=29
+ *     flexible_region rank=2 at=7d7bb0000 bytes=16384K prot=0x3 name=orbis
+ *     flexible_region rank=3 at=7d8bb0000 bytes=16384K prot=0x3 name=orbis
+ *
+ * -- the pool empties, and the next thread dies in pthread_create with EAGAIN
+ * at about a dozen, three layers from here, where it reads as a thread limit.
+ *
+ * So size the secondary arena for the budget it actually comes from. 8 MiB is
+ * a quarter of the PS5's and still four times the floor this header already
+ * considers survivable, and the threads it applies to are the ones the comment
+ * above calls an audio mixer or a loader -- far less code than the first
+ * thread, whose 128 MiB is untouched because that is the one a game's hot loop
+ * has to fit in.
+ *
+ * ⚠ A full arena is not fatal here either: the engine resets and translation
+ * continues. If a secondary thread ever turns out to run a real workload, this
+ * is the number to raise -- and the better answer is to put these back in
+ * direct memory, where the PS5 already keeps them and where this console has
+ * gigabytes rather than megabytes.
+ *
+ * ⚠ PW_WOW_PS4_BUDGET comes from scripts/prx-ps4.sh, not from __PROSPERO__:
+ * upstream that macro means a PS5, which is the console this file's 12 GiB was
+ * written for. */
+#define PW_WOW_OTHER_ARENA ((size_t)8 << 20)
+#else
 #define PW_WOW_OTHER_ARENA ((size_t)32 << 20)
+#endif
 #define PW_WOW_MIN_ARENA ((size_t)2 << 20)
 #define PW_WOW_FIRST_HOSTEXEC ((size_t)4 << 20)
 #define PW_WOW_OTHER_HOSTEXEC ((size_t)1 << 20)
